{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "CI + Standards Review After Task",
  "description": "After a spec task completes, runs the CI pipeline then reviews modified files for ownership, naming, and security.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "when": {
    "type": "postTaskExecution"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Run the following checks in order. Stop at the first failure.\n\n## Step 1 — CI Pipeline\n\n1. Run `npm run lint` — report pass/fail\n2. Run `npm test -- --run` — report pass/fail\n3. Run `npm run build` — report pass/fail\n\nIf any step fails, describe the error and suggest a fix. Do NOT proceed to Step 2.\n\n## Step 2 — File ownership (automated — do NOT eyeball this)\n\nOwnership is machine-checked. Do not re-derive the ownership list from memory; run the check:\n\n```\ngit diff --name-only --diff-filter=d > /tmp/changed-paths.txt\nnode .kiro/skills/shared/scope-check-ci.js /tmp/changed-paths.txt /tmp/labels.json\n```\n\nWrite the PR's `agent: *` labels to /tmp/labels.json as a JSON array first (e.g. `[\"agent: frontend\"]`); use `[]` if the PR does not exist yet. Exit 0 = in scope, 1 = violation, 2 = the ownership list itself is broken.\n\nReport its output verbatim. The single source of truth is the `OWNERSHIP` list in `.kiro/skills/pr-monitoring/review-parser-skill.js`, mirrored in the CONTRIBUTING.md table; the same check runs as the `Scope Check` CI job, so a clean result here predicts a green job. If it reports a path it cannot attribute, add a row to BOTH.\n\n## Step 3 — Standards Review\n\nThe remaining items still need judgement:\n\n1. Naming — kebab-case for .ts files, PascalCase for .tsx components\n2. Code smells — functions >20 lines, parameter lists >3, duplicated code\n3. Security — no hardcoded secrets, PII, or magic values\n4. Imports — flow inward toward shared/, no circular deps\n\nReport any violations found."
  }
}